Simulating Input-Based Attacks and Implementing Protections
Overview

This experiment demonstrates common web application vulnerabilities such as SQL Injection and XSS (Cross-Site Scripting) by simulating attacks on a test environment. It also implements security measures such as input validation and sanitization to prevent such attacks.

Objectives
Understand SQL Injection and XSS attack mechanisms
Perform controlled attacks on a test web application
Identify insecure input handling practices
Implement input validation and sanitization techniques

Tools Used
DVWA (Damn Vulnerable Web Application) / Local PHP Web App
XAMPP (Apache + MySQL)
Browser Developer Tools
VS Code

Methodology
Set up a vulnerable web application locally
Attempt SQL Injection via login input fields
Perform XSS attack using script injection in input fields
Observe system behavior and security flaws
Apply input validation and sanitization techniques
Re-test to verify protection effectiveness

Vulnerabilities Demonstrated
SQL Injection via unsanitized queries
Stored/Reflected XSS via input fields
Lack of server-side input validation

Countermeasures Implemented
Prepared SQL statements
Input sanitization using htmlspecialchars()
Server-side validation rules
Output encoding for safe rendering

Outcome

The experiment successfully demonstrated how insecure input handling leads to exploitation and how proper validation effectively mitigates these risks.

Conclusion

Input-based attacks remain one of the most common web vulnerabilities. Proper sanitization and secure coding practices significantly reduce attack surface and improve application security.